  "narrative": {
    "intro": "South Carolina has received $7.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 15,267 awards, with an average award size of $518K. Obligations fell 5.0% year over year. Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligations at $3.6B, followed by Remediation Services at $853M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $559M. The Department of Energy is the largest buyer in the state, obligating $4.5B so far this fiscal year. SAVANNAH RIVER NUCLEAR SOLUTIONS LLC is the top vendor, receiving $3.4B in obligations. Recent fiscal-year history shows a peak in FY24 at $10.6B, then a fall to $8.3B in FY25 and $7.9B in FY26 to date. Contractors focused on facilities, environmental remediation, construction, and engineering account for a large share of current-year spending. Buyers and prime vendors tied to the Savannah River mission dominate the top lists, reflecting concentrated program-level activity in the state.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting trended in South Carolina recently?",
        "body": "Obligations peaked at $10.6B in FY24 and then fell to $8.3B in FY25. Obligations continued to decline in FY26 to date at $7.9B, and obligations fell 5.0% year over year into the current fiscal year to date. Historical totals show steady growth from $6.7B in FY21 to $10.1B in FY23 before the recent decline. The Department of Energy’s $4.5B of obligations is a key driver of the current-year totals."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which program areas and contractors dominate federal spending in South Carolina?",
        "body": "Facilities Support Services leads industries with $3.6B in obligations, and Remediation Services follows with $853M. SAVANNAH RIVER NUCLEAR SOLUTIONS LLC received $3.4B and SAVANNAH RIVER MISSION COMPLETION, LLC received $835M, showing concentration among Savannah River contractors. Department-level buying is concentrated: the Department of Energy obligated $4.5B and the Department of Defense obligated $1.8B in FY2026 to date."
      }
    ]
  },
